-- title:
--   Étale entry of Uₚ on the special fibre of J₁(Mp)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$.
--
--   **Arithmetic setting.** $L$ is a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, $\zeta \in L$ is a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ with `hK` asserting that $K$ is the Laurent base change [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise images of the $q$-expansion field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ is in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), and $K$ is an $A$-algebra in a way compatible with $L$. Finally $j \in K$ is a nonzero element whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the image of the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-function (`hj`). The scheme [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) is the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the $A$-algebras of elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$, respectively over $A[j^{-1}]$, and [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) is its structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, assumed proper.
--
--   **Geometric special fibre.** $k$ is an algebraically closed $A$-algebra field of characteristic $p$. $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and $i_1, i_2$ are closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ over $k$ into the base change of the two-chart model to $k$. The hypothesis `hcover` says that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` says that the scheme-theoretic intersection $C_1 \times_X C_2$ is reduced; $n$ is its number of points (`hn`), assumed positive (`hn0`). Sections $\varepsilon$ of the two-chart model over $A$, and $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ of $c_1$, $c_2$ over $k$, are given, with `hε₁` saying that $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ is the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$.
--
--   **Relative Picard data.** A `RelativePic0Designation` over $A$ consists of a scheme $D.P$, a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a section $D.\mathrm{zeroSection}$ of it. $D$ is such a designation for the two-chart model, and `hrep` asserts that it represents, via a Poincaré rigidified line bundle, the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles on the relative curve which are fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero (`algEquivZeroCut`); $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ is smooth (`hsm`) and separated (`hsep`). The hypothesis `hreps` gives the corresponding representability over $k$ for the base change $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k$, and `hPk` identifies its Poincaré bundle with the base change of the Poincaré bundle of $D$. Designations $D_1$, $D_2$ over $k$ for $c_1$, $c_2$, with representability statements `hrep₁`, `hrep₂`, are given, together with a morphism $\nu_2$ over $k$ from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$; `hν₂` says that for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every section $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $t$, the pullback of the $C_2$-Poincaré bundle along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification (along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and the second projection) of the restriction along $i_2$ of the pullback of the $k$-Poincaré bundle along $a$; thus $\nu_2$ realises restriction of line bundles to $C_2$.
--
--   **Geometric generic fibre.** $M\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} = \mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb{Q}$ of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), $e\eta$ an isomorphism from $M\eta.C$ onto the base change of the two-chart model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`), the preimage of the finite chart being nonempty. The hypothesis `hMηpin` says that each element $a$ of the finite chart algebra `chartAlgFin A K j`, viewed through $e\eta$ as a germ at the generic point and transported by $M\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}$, has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image of that of $a$ under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. The hypothesis `hgal` is Galois equivariance: for every $g \in \operatorname{Aut}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing $L$ pointwise and every two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M\eta.C$, if $x'$ followed by the chart projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$ followed by that projection, then the place attached to $x'$ is the image of the place attached to $x$ under the semilinear automorphism [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54) of $g$.
--
--   **Special-fibre group datum.** $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus} \le G.J0s$ and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J0s \to G.JI \times G.JE$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J0s$, $G.JI$, $G.JE$ with the $k$-sections of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ respectively; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` say that these bijections are additive in the sense that the Poincaré pullback along a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks; `hproj` says that for every $x \in G.J0s$ the $G.JI$-component of $G.\mathrm{proj}\,x$ corresponds to `pts x` composed with the morphism of designations induced by $i_1$, and the $G.JE$-component to `pts x` composed with $\nu_2$.
--
--   **Generic points and Hecke action.** `gpts` is a bijection between $\mathrm{JOne}(Mp) = \mathrm{Pic}^0_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` and the sections of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, additive for the relative group law attached to `hrep` (`hgadd`). $\varphi$ assigns to each element $t$ of the Hecke algebra [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) an endomorphism of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $A$; `hφmul` says each $\varphi(t)$ is a homomorphism for the relative group law, and `hφpts` that, for the Hecke module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129), composition with $\varphi(t)$ computes the action of $t$ on $\mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$ through `gpts`.
--
--   **Abel–Jacobi package.** The hypotheses `hDL`, `ajL`, `kL`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hPL`, `hajLε`, `hajL`, `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_aj` provide: representability of the relative Picard functor after base change to $L$ together with the identification `hPL` of its Poincaré bundle; an Abel–Jacobi morphism `ajL` over $L$ carrying the section $\varepsilon$ to the zero section (`hajLε`) and characterised by `hajL`, which states that for every field $K'$, every $K'$-point $x$ of the $L$-curve the Poincaré pullback along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of $\varepsilon$; the comparison morphism `kL` between the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$- and $L$-base changes, compatible with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`); the induced morphism `ajbar` on $M\eta.C$ (`hajbar`, `hajbar_over`); a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point `εbar` of $M\eta.C$ lying over $\varepsilon$ (`hεbar`) and sent to the zero section (`hεbar_aj`); and `hpts_aj`, which states that for any two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, s$ of $M\eta.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$ there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ equal to $[\,\mathrm{place}(x)\,] - [\,\mathrm{place}(s)\,]$ whose class satisfies $\mathrm{gpts}(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of }Dv) = x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   **Igusa identification of $C_1$, and Frobenius.** $w$ is an [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16) (a weight-one form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction to $k$ is nonzero), and `igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w` is the field obtained from the $q$-expansion field of $X_1(M)$ over $k$ by adjoining the inverse of that reduction. $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ is a curve model of this field over $k$, $e_1$ an isomorphism $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`), the relevant chart preimage being nonempty; `hgauss₁` states the Gauss-type reduction rule: whenever $a$ in the finite chart algebra satisfies $a \cdot y = x$ for power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ having nonzero reduction in $k$, the function attached to $a$ on $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ has Laurent expansion the quotient of the reductions of $x$ and $y$. $\theta_1$ is an isomorphism of $G.JI$ with $\mathrm{Pic}^0_k$ of the Igusa field, computed on points by `hθpin₁`: if the Poincaré pullback at `ptsI g` is isomorphic to the line bundle of a $k$-point $x$ of $c_1$ tensored with the ideal module of $\varepsilon_1$, then $\theta_1 g$ is the class of the divisor $[x] - [\varepsilon_1]$ read through $e_1$. The semilinear automorphism `frobIg` of the Igusa field over $k$ raises every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power (`hfrobIg`).
--
--   **Auxiliary group subscheme.** $a : \mathcal{A} \to \operatorname{Spec} A$ is proper (`h𝒜pr`) and smooth (`h𝒜sm`) with connected geometric fibres (`h𝒜conn`), and $\iota$ is a closed immersion (`h𝒜cl`) of $\mathcal{A}$ over $A$ into $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ whose image is stable under the relative group law, in the sense that the unit, products and inverses of points coming from $\mathcal{A}$ again come from $\mathcal{A}$ (`h𝒜grp`), and stable under each $\varphi(t)$ (`h𝒜hecke`); `h𝒜gen` says that an element $x$ of $\mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt (M * p) p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) if and only if $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ factors through $\iota$.
--
--   **Place and reduction data.** $\mathrm{Pl}$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ in its nonunits (`hPl`), $\rho : A \to \mathrm{Pl}$ is compatible with $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`), $O$ is a subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ contained in $\mathrm{Pl}$ (`hO`) with a compatible $\rho_O : A \to O$ (`hρO`), and $\pi_k : \mathrm{Pl} \to k$ is a surjective ring homomorphism (`hπk`) with $A \to k$ equal to $\pi_k \circ \rho$ (`hAlgk`).
--
--   **The Atkin–Lehner automorphism.** $\sigma$ is an $L$-automorphism of $K$ such that the Laurent expansion of $\sigma j$ is the image of $j(q^p)$ (`hσj`); $\sigma$ preserves the finite chart algebra in both directions (`hσfin`); for every valuation subring $W_0$ of $K$ whose elements are exactly those $f$ admitting power series $x, y$ over $A$ with $y$ of nonzero reduction and $f \cdot y = x$, the $\sigma$-pullback of $W_0$ differs from $W_0$ and contains, together with their inverses, all values $P(j)$ for polynomials $P$ over $A$ of nonzero reduction (`hσW`); on the full modular function field of level $Mp$, $\sigma$ induces the Atkin–Lehner involution [`ModularCurve.atkinLehnerInvolutionFull M p`](def/ModularCurve_AtkinLehnerPartial.html#L21) (`hσAL`); and `hdiamConj` states the diamond conjugation law: for $d, d'$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d' \equiv d \pmod M$ and $d'd \equiv 1 \pmod p$, and for automorphisms $\theta_d$, $\theta_{d'}$ of $K$ inducing the diamond automorphisms $\langle d \rangle$, $\langle d' \rangle$ of `x1FunctionField (M * p)` after base change, $\sigma \theta_d \sigma^{-1}$ and $\theta_{d'}$ agree on $K$ at the level of Laurent expansions. $\bar\sigma$ is the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-automorphism of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` induced by $\sigma$ (`hσbar`).
--
--   **Igusa identification of $C_2$.** $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$, $e_2$, `he₂`, the chart nonemptiness, `hgauss₂`, $\theta_2$ and `hθpin₂` are the analogues for $C_2$ of the corresponding data for $C_1$, with `hgauss₂` expressing the reduction rule for $\sigma(a)$ rather than $a$; thus $\theta_2 : G.JE \cong \mathrm{Pic}^0_k$ of the Igusa field is the Picard identification of the second component, twisted by $\sigma$.
--
--   **Hecke inputs at $p$ and sorting.** `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58). $U_s$ is an endomorphism of $G.J0s$ with `hUsA`: for every $y$, the $k$-point `pts (Us y)`, read in the two-chart model, is `pts y` followed by $\varphi$ of the Hecke generator at $p$. Further data: an integer $d$ coprime to $Mp$ (`hd`), a $k$-automorphism $\delta$ of the Igusa field, a finite set $S_0$ of places of the Igusa field, and a map $\mathrm{red}_2$ from places of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` to places of the Igusa field characterised by `hred₂`: if a place $P$ is carried by a section $\xi$ of the two-chart model over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$ whose $\mathrm{Pl}$-fibre is the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $P$, and if a $k$-point $c$ of $c_2$ reduces $\xi$ via $\pi_k$, then $\mathrm{red}_2 P$ is the place of $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$ attached to $c$. The integrality hypotheses `hβdef`, `hα`, `hβ` make the Hecke correspondence [`ModularCurve.heckeDivOneBar`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeOperator.html#L151) at $\ell = p$ available on divisors, and principal divisors are assumed to exist for the relevant $X_1/X_0$ function field.
--
--   Here a place is said to have *good $C_2$-data* when there exist $\xi$ as above and a $k$-point $c$ of $c_2$ with $\mathrm{Pl}$-fibre of $\xi$ the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of the place, $c$ reducing $\xi$ via $\pi_k$, the image of $c$ avoiding the crossing locus (the image of the second projection of $C_1 \times_X C_2$), and the image of $c$ in the two-chart model lying in the finite chart; *good $C_1$-data* is defined in the same way with $i_1$, $c_1$ and the first projection of $C_1 \times_X C_2$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hDIA` states: for places $P, P_2$ with $P_2 = \langle d \rangle \cdot P$ (the action of `ofAlgAut (diamondAutBar (M * p) d)`), if $P$ has good $C_2$-data then so does $P_2$, and $\mathrm{red}_2 P_2 = \delta \cdot \mathrm{red}_2 P$. The hypothesis `hREAD` states: for places $P, P_2, Q_p$ with $P_2 = \langle d \rangle \cdot P$, if $P$ and $Q_p$ both have good $C_2$-data, $\mathrm{red}_2 P \notin S_0$, and $Q_p$ lies in the support of `heckeDivOneBar` applied to $[P]$, then $\mathrm{red}_2 Q_p = \mathrm{frobIg} \cdot \mathrm{red}_2 P_2$. Finally, with $S_b$ a further finite set of places of the Igusa field, `hSort` states: for every place $P$ with good $C_2$-data and $\mathrm{red}_2 P \notin S_b$, there are a place $Q_p$ and a family $Q : \mathrm{Fin}(p-1) \to$ places with `heckeDivOneBar` applied to $[P]$ equal to $[Q_p] + \sum_{i} [Q_i]$, with $Q_p$ having good $C_2$-data and each $Q_i$ having good $C_1$-data.
--
--   **The payload point.** $P$ and $P'$ are places of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`, $zz$ is a section of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho)$, and $t \in G.J0s$. The divisor $[P] - [P']$ has degree zero (`hDv`); every place in its support has good $C_2$-data (`hSUPP`); the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $\mathrm{gpts}$ of the class of $[P] - [P']$ is the $\mathrm{Pl}$-fibre of $zz$ (`hgen`); the $k$-point `pts t`, read in the two-chart model, is the reduction of $zz$ along $\pi_k$ (`hredt`); and $\mathrm{red}_2 P$, $\mathrm{red}_2 P'$ lie neither in $S_0$ nor in $S_b$ (`hSP₀`, `hSPb`, `hSP₀'`, `hSPb'`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** The $G.JE$-component of $G.\mathrm{proj}(U_s\,t)$ equals
--   $$(G.\mathrm{proj}(U_s\,t))_2 = \theta_2^{-1}\bigl( (\mathrm{frobIg} \cdot \mathrm{ofAlgAut}\,\delta) \cdot \theta_2\bigl((G.\mathrm{proj}\,t)_2\bigr) \bigr),$$
--   where the product is taken in the group of semilinear automorphisms of the Igusa function field over $k$ (pairs consisting of a ring automorphism of the field and one of $k$, compatible with the structure map; $\mathrm{ofAlgAut}\,\delta$ is the pair $(\delta, 1)$), acting on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ through its action on places.
--
--   This is the étale part of the Eichler–Shimura relation for $U_p$ on the Néron special fibre of $J_1(Mp)$: on the Igusa component $C_2$, read through the Picard identification $\theta_2$, the operator $U_p$ acts as Frobenius composed with a diamond operator realised by the $k$-automorphism $\delta$. It is stated for a single payload point $t$ arising from a pair of places $P, P'$ with good reduction into $C_2$, and is used by the existence statement which produces a $d$ coprime to $Mp$, an automorphism $\delta$ and finite exceptional sets for which both components of $G.\mathrm{proj}$ obey the corresponding formulae.
